cff-version: 1.2.0
title: >-
  Reinforcement Learning for Molecular Lead Optimization: exploration
  policies, and what has to be true before they can be compared
message: >-
  If you refer to this work, please cite it using the metadata below.
type: software
authors:
  - given-names: Vlad
    family-names: Navitski
    orcid: https://orcid.org/0000-0002-6620-0123
abstract: >-
  A preliminary study comparing five reinforcement-learning exploration
  policies — Random, epsilon-greedy, triggered epsilon-greedy, UCB and
  Thompson Sampling — on EGFR inhibitor lead optimization using ChEMBL
  bioactivity data. No learned policy outperformed random modification.
  The study's main output is an account of the constraints that make that
  comparison hard to run at all: a total-ordering defect that made the seed
  pool platform-dependent, amplification of last-bit floating-point
  differences through the reinforcement-learning feedback loop, and a single
  linear weight matrix asked to represent every scaffold family at once.
keywords:
  - reinforcement learning
  - drug discovery
  - lead optimization
  - QSAR
  - cheminformatics
  - reproducibility
  - EGFR
